#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183865 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "563087c2-8d87-5b95-833d-f9892d9276a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10535" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\arccos(x) -\\pi}{x-\\pi}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\arccos(x){:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)-\\pi }{x-\\pi }) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)-\\pi }{0-\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi )}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183866 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"}] :question/id #uuid "c1ba0b9f-78d6-5791-8816-b71da089e57f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10536" :question/problem "What is the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y = \\arccos(x-1){:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/4ccf7e79e19f49027f615a075e655139e6926b3ba30fe35d63078fd7917d8869.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x-1)$ is $x \\in [0,2].$ This means that $\\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183867 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/id #uuid "7ce46ca9-cfff-5e8b-89b9-a2a7b34921fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10537" :question/problem "Compute the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty}\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2}{3}\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right)\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right){:}$  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/06d01e3eb47f69e5172e0ba3c99708a05c79b12a48764fe29c50e94f93b73dd1.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(2x+1 \\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty,$ the graph tends to $y={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd1e50c5-17fd-54ac-b5e9-5272c6c9f1a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181209} {:answer/id #uuid "102902b1-548b-5d51-b2da-f0a9a049550f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181210} {:answer/id #uuid "2ade5223-3c88-566d-8688-d20828e0d736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181211} {:answer/id #uuid "62733b1f-741b-56cd-ab26-ba7562c7f90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181212} {:answer/id #uuid "652b0975-5b45-5ba0-87d7-092747c2da4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ade5223-3c88-566d-8688-d20828e0d736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181211} :answer-field/id #uuid "36265b7b-d7ba-500f-b44d-b2d731abc742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53588a2b-ad7c-5261-a3ca-c56ccdc03a42" :question/math-academy-id "q-149958" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c3c1289-a879-573d-9eb3-d65f1d37b731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285934} {:answer/id #uuid "4418da1c-6be9-5ba4-b93d-a9c265f63aa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285935} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1dd7c9-6c1b-5cd4-af99-7b05fd67350a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285936} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c4a592-aab5-5686-8cd5-4e6113bbf43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285937} {:answer/id #uuid "beb8dbb1-7437-5e44-853a-b4cc172eddba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "beb8dbb1-7437-5e44-853a-b4cc172eddba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285938} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6dd5e83-24c1-5120-bd45-0ede9e9579cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78b11030-53ab-5210-86a9-a47f5842bef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-152757" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "405a0393-dfe6-5fa6-856b-5f75849de079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285941} {:answer/id #uuid "898acdac-0565-5a62-a0f4-c46f577f7c72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285942} {:answer/id #uuid "1acffa2b-2844-5fa6-8971-3c4a3f57b777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285943} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d1ab36-d749-502f-af54-3c9e18f610ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285944} {:answer/id #uuid "5b875200-3e3e-5467-a05e-f10504f61faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b875200-3e3e-5467-a05e-f10504f61faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285945} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c046085-8ed4-528a-957c-e01f7dd16be2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c6ad1c8-a588-5c5c-9221-f6b581281b6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-152761" :question/problem "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3/2}\\frac{(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)-\\pi )^{2}}{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since $\\frac{3}{2}\\notin [-1,1],$ this means that $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})$ is undefined.\n\nTherefore, our limit is undefined, too."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebc48fde-e5f6-542a-ba01-2e35b421cc11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285948} {:answer/id #uuid "f205aa08-d49d-5ee0-9ba0-7ef458d8fae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285949} {:answer/id #uuid "2c5e5b2b-3ea5-575a-87e0-50975db2f3b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285950} {:answer/id #uuid "16f10d8d-aea1-5d45-ae42-fb83850398c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285951} {:answer/id #uuid "d830f0f0-ca80-5598-948a-75ecc06df018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c5e5b2b-3ea5-575a-87e0-50975db2f3b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285950} :answer-field/id #uuid "4133f92f-6aae-55c9-9f09-9a13186434c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e816e4b-b356-5e73-aed4-0d7b397c41f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-152804" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$   \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}/2$ directly into the limit, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\cdot \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ba3568b-28c4-5f11-a150-4bdbc80c45af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285955} {:answer/id #uuid "07756689-fa59-582e-b7d1-e710c27f93de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285956} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c0c578-73de-567b-8c16-f60ae47f1f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285957} {:answer/id #uuid "869f78b8-3248-5a18-ad73-a7ab0e1a85d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285958} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9c4f12-0908-5c52-a26b-d011d1ba781f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ba3568b-28c4-5f11-a150-4bdbc80c45af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285955} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd8a9876-8b94-545e-9df8-3cbbe2dbb301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f0395ad-4116-54b1-9ef2-77603b0cd9e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-152806" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2+2x)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2+2x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/2ba0e6cf85e73ee89063e487611a64292a21496f93ef2457ae5e5b15cb1478b3.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2+2x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2+2x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2+2x)=3\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98333dda-488e-541d-a2a7-6d03bfad08f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285962} {:answer/id #uuid "1a14a5aa-0a11-5ed9-a0e9-c84913ac858a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285963} {:answer/id #uuid "503e3a87-12bc-5325-969c-8c82adbc5fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285964} {:answer/id #uuid "f13f78cf-e357-5dc0-ac84-7d47d529bcc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285965} {:answer/id #uuid "876a7085-835f-5e6e-8af2-f9fad1791ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "876a7085-835f-5e6e-8af2-f9fad1791ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285966} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc0aaf96-6e5e-51ea-8ef4-572459801376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "972afef4-ce2e-5e29-97e1-db6ccb0c6cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-152869" :question/problem "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2}):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/922781004fc917f3a62726689c43f0226c680de58f446b741f9994c4c65134e3.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2})$ is $x\\in [-3,1].$ This means that $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2})$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2})$ is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b935fc7-e380-5114-9e52-0215a4878d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285969} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc0b939-946e-50e3-9f15-0b30a3a84de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285970} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8c5cb1-143c-5819-9abf-b855d8e43d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285971} {:answer/id #uuid "23d16969-d38b-5bc3-9961-b8ecdc223bd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285972} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b78d40-84e8-5131-9ee1-6b308e050d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8b78d40-84e8-5131-9ee1-6b308e050d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285973} :answer-field/id #uuid "73062c0b-1bb1-5675-b20d-b02fcea42f5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a8abbc2-0426-5776-9148-d32d6510319e" :question/math-academy-id "q-152890" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6272e48-c1e2-500f-932f-a35a5eeeabb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6272e48-c1e2-500f-932f-a35a5eeeabb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285976} :answer-field/id #uuid "af1c7750-e9ab-5d3d-bedf-109e79123943" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f502e27-aa98-50c3-b27e-affffdb3b3fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-302650" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{\\pi x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{\\pi x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)}{\\pi \\cdot (1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1cdfa3-e161-5c97-9a8f-c0c88c352ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f1cdfa3-e161-5c97-9a8f-c0c88c352ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285979} :answer-field/id #uuid "547b70b6-5a17-574a-87a0-e699a471d542" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53aec58a-1622-5fa5-804d-afee9e1d5fc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-302690" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bf337a8-b5a4-5a83-81db-d81ff85a0333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bf337a8-b5a4-5a83-81db-d81ff85a0333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285982} :answer-field/id #uuid "53222422-9b67-56e6-a4ab-c741881611f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ad685fe-8879-588e-8c60-0099d11bdb00" :question/math-academy-id "q-339131" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(2\\sqrt{3}x+6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$   \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(2\\sqrt{3}x+6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =2\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+6\\cdot \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3+6\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c09bf845-0c17-56ce-91e4-ed1095c438c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285985} {:answer/id #uuid "750c8ae8-5259-5d94-92ff-f9c084bf81f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c09bf845-0c17-56ce-91e4-ed1095c438c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285985} :answer-field/id #uuid "be1245d4-cc61-52d9-8325-23f44a77d608" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ca39296-785a-5b9f-bd41-07df813f9b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339330" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/e441953c2cefc6f817a64cb7ce0ebce53164ebb4d3974ae871b14d57450b5167.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1))=\\pi -(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb4f3204-1b07-538d-b8d1-8339a35f8fe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285989} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0c7a81-7641-59c0-9233-16662e5f0148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb0c7a81-7641-59c0-9233-16662e5f0148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285990} :answer-field/id #uuid "627bffb8-8132-5eb6-8964-5eecd1ce4523" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b3afa35b-eeeb-5afa-aaf1-a5819291da16" :question/math-academy-id "q-339500" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/f5cef3bda76e80edfa467a90823c9b6ad6aca1f8a5e8e3e06dc585258e975e0a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "534a8fdb-e3a5-5676-bcda-b855af89c014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285993} {:answer/id #uuid "5ddc20fd-4516-57a7-a1d0-01a868571065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ddc20fd-4516-57a7-a1d0-01a868571065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285994} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe5e9c2a-8fbb-56e0-bc94-88f9a3f0fc4e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e9bd242-8a9c-523c-836e-941311447505" :question/math-academy-id "q-343173" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{7}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/04dd8257ef32291b930aa29f5bed4de2c41761692fd48d08ed2479f6cbd78659.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{7}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x))=\\frac{7}{\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 1